Type casting refers to the conversion of data from one type to another. It is of two types.
i) primitive ii) non-primmtive

Primitive type casting is also of two types.
a) Widening: When we convert a smaller data type to a larger data type, it is called as widening. The larger and smaller refers to the memory size they occupy.
It occurs implicitly.
Example: int a = 5;     //a -> 5
         dounle b = a;  //a -> 5.0
b) Narrowing: It is the conversion of a larger data type to a smaller one. It occurs explicitly.

Some orders of increasing memory sizes of data types are as follows.
byte -> short -> int -> long -> float -> double
char -> int -> long -> float -> double

Non-primitive type casting is also of two types. It is also called as derived casting or class type casting. Is-A relationship is mandatory for non-primitive type casting.
a) Upcasting: When we create an object of a sub-class and it is stored with a reference of super class. It is called as Upcasting. It occurs implicitly.
Example: Father f = new Son();
Using the upcasted reference we can only access the super class properties not the sub class properties.

b) Downcasting: It involves the conversion of the reference of upcasted object from the super class back to the sub class reference. It occurs explicitly.
Example: Son s = (Son)f;
After downcasting using the reference (e.g. s), we can access both sub-class and super class properties.

Upcasting and downcasting is used for generalization means to access properties of unrelated classes using a single super class that has a Is-A relationship with 
all the sub classes.
